<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.11.4: Writing Quadratics When Given Any Format</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02268</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.11.4: Writing Quadratics When Given Any Format</md:title>
    <md:uuid>4c88c41d-443c-4387-9bae-fea866b83b3a</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Cool Down (5 minutes)</title>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>In this lesson, you have learned how to write a quadratic function from different starting contexts and when provided different information.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify a quadratic function in standard form, \(b(x)\), with the zeros –6 and 8.</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \(b(x) = x^2 − 2x − 48\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>Identify a quadratic function in standard form, \(m(x)\), with the given zero.</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt="A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UP WITH AN \(x\)-intercepts OF 6."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b3c2e79ea248f2e456cd3375fb701cf47b884168"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Answer: </emphasis>\(m(x) = x^2 − 12x + 36\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Write the specific quadratic function, \(d(x)\), in standard form, that has the zeros 2 and 5 and passes through the point \((1, 16)\).</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Intercept form: \(d(x) = a(x − 2)(x − 5)\)</para>
<para>Find the value of \(a\). Use point \((1, 16)\).</para>
<para>\(d(x) = a(x − 2)(x − 5)\)
  \(16 = a((1) − 2)((1) − 5)\)
  \(16 = a(-1)(-4)\)
  \(16 = 4a\)
  \(4 = a\)</para>
<para>Substitute 4 for \(a\).</para>
<para>\(d(x) = a(x − 2)(x − 5)\)
  \(d(x) = 4(x − 2)(x − 5)\)
  \(d(x) = 4(x^2 − 2x − 5x + 10)\)
  \(d(x) = 4(x^2 − 7x + 10)\)
  \(d(x) = 4x^2 − 28x + 40\)</para>
<para>So, the specific quadratic function written in standard form is \(d(x) = 4x^2 − 28x + 40\).</para>

<title>Response to Student Thinking</title>
<title>More chances </title>
<para>Students will have more opportunities to understand the mathematical ideas in this cool down, so there is no need to slow down or add additional work to the next lessons. Instead, use the results of this cool down to provide guidance for what to look for and emphasize in the next several lessons to support students in advancing their current understanding.</para>
</section>
</content>
